00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:43.825
The grocery checkout: cart, then price and promo validation, then stock reservation in the customer's dark store, then payment, then order confirmation, with reservations that expire, dark-store stock that doesn't match the shelf, and 8 pm flash sales.

10
00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:59.145
Earlier chapters deferred the hard parts here: the national single-SKU deal on one hot row (Ch 02, Ch 05), bucketing and a Redis front-gate, and checkout re-validates availability (Ch 22).

11
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:03.969
L1: the 1 rupeesonion deal (real PostgreSQL + Redis).

12
00:01:04.249 --> 00:01:08.324
5,000 units, 20,000 attempts, 64 workers.

13
00:01:09.674 --> 00:01:19.594
One row with an atomic conditional UPDATE sold out in 6.1 s (~820 per second, p99 254 ms).

14
00:01:19.874 --> 00:01:21.581
SELECT FOR UPDATE was slower.

15
00:01:21.861 --> 00:01:26.789
16 buckets sold out 4.7x faster (1.29 s).

16
00:01:27.069 --> 00:01:30.653
Without a sold-out flag, though, the losers probed every bucket:

17
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:35.391
245,489 DB transactions.

18
00:01:35.671 --> 00:01:41.218
A Redis front-gate kept the database to 5,000 transactions, yet sold out no faster.

19
00:01:41.498 --> 00:01:43.631
All four designs oversold 0.

20
00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:45.895
L2: how long to hold stock.

21
00:01:46.175 --> 00:01:50.698
A flash sale of 10,000 boxes for 25,000 customers.

22
00:01:52.048 --> 00:02:00.987
A 30-minute hold left 1,621 boxes unsold while 10,897 customers were told sold out.

23
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:07.603
A 3-minute hold sold everything but charged 460 slow payers for boxes already gone.

24
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:16.736
Holding 3 minutes and extending to 15 once payment starts sold 9,993 with 123 late captures.

25
00:02:17.016 --> 00:02:18.637
L3: phantom stock.

26
00:02:18.917 --> 00:02:24.229
Recorded stock overstated the shelf by 1,619 units in one day.

27
00:02:24.509 --> 00:02:29.715
Selling to recorded stock left 3.91% of orders with a missing item.

28
00:02:31.065 --> 00:02:37.721
A flat 2-unit buffer cut that to 1.23% but hid 2,160 real units.

29
00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:48.667
A per-SKU buffer learned from pick failures, plus correcting the record on a failed pick, reached 0.40%, hiding 1,509 units.

30
00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:54.835
L4: the 8 pm crowd, end to end (real HTTP + PostgreSQL).

31
00:02:55.115 --> 00:02:59.169
8,000 customers arrived in 2 s for 2,000 boxes.

32
00:02:59.449 --> 00:03:10.115
With no admission control, 7,575 got no answer in 25 s, and 1,838 buyers never heard that they'd bought.

33
00:03:11.465 --> 00:03:19.060
A fast-503 limit answered everyone, but only 31% of buyers were among the first 2,000 to arrive.

34
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:25.441
A virtual waiting room answered everyone, and 92% of buyers were among the first 2,000.

35
00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:26.703
