38
00:03:31.258 --> 00:03:33.178
Introduction and Why It Matters.

39
00:03:34.428 --> 00:03:35.345
A story:

40
00:03:35.625 --> 00:03:37.119
Diwali at 8 pm.

41
00:03:37.399 --> 00:03:43.479
Marketing announced 1 rupeesonions, 5,000 kg, 8 pm tonight across India.

42
00:03:43.759 --> 00:03:47.321
The checkout team had load-tested for 3x normal traffic.

43
00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:49.991
At 8:00:00:

44
00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:52.809
One row became the whole company's bottleneck.

45
00:03:53.089 --> 00:03:56.759
Every checkout with onions tried to decrement the same stock row.

46
00:03:57.039 --> 00:04:04.697
Checkouts for customers who didn't even want onions slowed down, because the connection pool was full of transactions queued on that row.

47
00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.684
Timeouts turned into retries.

48
00:04:06.964 --> 00:04:11.700
Apps timed out after 3 seconds and retried; the retries joined the same queue.

49
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.015
The service was busy completing checkouts for people who had already given up and tapped again.

50
00:04:18.365 --> 00:04:20.157
Sold out while carts held stock.

51
00:04:20.437 --> 00:04:24.383
The first 5,000 checkouts reserved onions for 30 minutes.

52
00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:32.109
Thousands abandoned, but their reservations kept the onions off sale until 8:30, long after the crowd had left.

53
00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.626
Charged for nothing.

54
00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:41.863
Customers whose UPI app took a long time paid after their reservation had expired and the onions had gone to someone else.

55
00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:43.679
Missing items at the door.

56
00:04:43.959 --> 00:04:47.159
For fresh produce, recorded stock overstated the shelf.

57
00:04:47.439 --> 00:04:53.498
Pickers couldn't find items that checkout had happily sold; customers got partial orders and refunds.

58
00:04:54.848 --> 00:04:55.595
Unfair.

59
00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:59.651
Who got the deal depended on retry timing, not on who came first.

60
00:04:59.931 --> 00:05:01.467
Social media noticed.

61
00:05:01.747 --> 00:05:15.613
This chapter designs the checkout that survives 8 pm: fast and correct stock reservation under contention, reservations with sensible lifetimes, honesty about physical stock, and admission control that is fair.

62
00:05:16.963 --> 00:05:17.646
Quote:

63
00:05:17.926 --> 00:05:19.590
The thesis of this chapter.

64
00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:26.590
Checkout is where scarce physical stock meets unbounded demand, and every design choice decides who gets what.

65
00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:37.387
Contention on hot items is solved by spreading the lock (buckets), keeping losers away from the database (a gate) and never holding locks across round trips (L1).

66
00:05:37.667 --> 00:05:46.393
Reservations are leases with a business-chosen lifetime; extend on evidence of intent, and handle late payments explicitly (L2).

67
00:05:46.673 --> 00:05:53.478
Recorded stock is an estimate of the shelf; buffer where it's wrong and correct it from the picker's feedback (L3).

68
00:05:53.758 --> 00:06:00.051
And under a crowd, admission control decides both whether the system survives and whether it's fair (L4).

