189
00:11:59.123 --> 00:12:01.896
Holding a row lock across round trips (SELECT...

190
00:12:02.176 --> 00:12:04.693
FOR UPDATE, then UPDATE) lengthens the queue.

191
00:12:04.973 --> 00:12:13.656
Buckets split stock into N rows; each reservation picks one (randomly or by customer hash) and falls back to others when it's empty.

192
00:12:13.936 --> 00:12:15.621
Lock throughput scales with N.

193
00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:19.165
The cost: deciding sold out means checking every bucket.

194
00:12:19.445 --> 00:12:29.621
Cache a sold-out flag (or a total counter) once all buckets reach zero, or losers keep probing, as L1's 245k transactions show.

195
00:12:29.901 --> 00:12:33.379
Rebalance buckets near the end so no bucket strands stock.

196
00:12:34.729 --> 00:12:42.899
A Redis gate (Lua decrement-if-positive) answers winners and losers in microseconds and keeps losers away from the database.

197
00:12:43.179 --> 00:12:51.137
It is a second copy of the count: seed it from the database, and reconcile it, because a crash between gate and database leaks stock.

198
00:12:51.417 --> 00:12:56.430
It doesn't speed up the winners, who still queue on the database row, combine it with buckets.

199
00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:04.795
Queued reservations: put reservation requests on a partitioned queue and have a single consumer per SKU apply them in order.

200
00:13:05.075 --> 00:13:08.617
That's throughput without lock contention, with asynchronous answers.

201
00:13:09.967 --> 00:13:19.908
Keep the reservation transaction tiny: no payment calls, no remote calls, only the stock decrement and the reservation insert (Ch 05).

202
00:13:21.258 --> 00:13:22.879
Reservations as leases.

203
00:13:23.159 --> 00:13:28.194
A reservation is a lease: stock held for a customer until an expiry time.

204
00:13:28.474 --> 00:13:29.882
Model it explicitly:

205
00:13:31.232 --> 00:13:32.832
