613
00:43:47.009 --> 00:43:48.716
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

614
00:43:49.966 --> 00:43:50.883
Watch out:

615
00:43:51.163 --> 00:43:51.803
1.

616
00:43:52.083 --> 00:43:53.790
Read-check-write stock updates.

617
00:43:54.070 --> 00:43:57.441
Race conditions oversell; use a conditional UPDATE.

618
00:43:58.791 --> 00:43:59.708
Watch out:

619
00:43:59.988 --> 00:44:00.692
2.

620
00:44:00.972 --> 00:44:03.873
Payment calls inside the reservation transaction.

621
00:44:04.153 --> 00:44:05.625
Locks held for seconds.

622
00:44:06.975 --> 00:44:07.893
Watch out:

623
00:44:08.173 --> 00:44:08.919
3.

624
00:44:09.199 --> 00:44:17.242
One row for a national deal. ~800 per second and a 250 ms p99 for everyone (L1).

625
00:44:18.592 --> 00:44:19.509
Watch out:

626
00:44:19.789 --> 00:44:20.493
4.

627
00:44:20.773 --> 00:44:22.629
Buckets without a sold-out flag.

628
00:44:22.909 --> 00:44:25.555
Losers probe every bucket forever (L1).

629
00:44:26.905 --> 00:44:27.822
Watch out:

630
00:44:28.102 --> 00:44:28.870
5.

631
00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:31.070
Treating the Redis gate as the truth.

632
00:44:31.350 --> 00:44:33.334
It drifts; the database decides.

633
00:44:34.684 --> 00:44:35.601
Watch out:

634
00:44:35.881 --> 00:44:36.628
6.

635
00:44:36.908 --> 00:44:39.276
Long reservation holds in flash sales.

636
00:44:39.556 --> 00:44:42.201
Stock stranded in abandoned carts (L2).

637
00:44:43.551 --> 00:44:44.469
Watch out:

638
00:44:44.749 --> 00:44:45.495
7.

639
00:44:45.775 --> 00:44:47.226
No late-capture path.

640
00:44:47.506 --> 00:44:50.237
Customers charged for items that are gone (L2).

641
00:44:51.587 --> 00:44:52.504
Watch out:

642
00:44:52.784 --> 00:44:53.467
8.

643
00:44:53.747 --> 00:44:55.304
Believing recorded stock.

644
00:44:55.584 --> 00:44:58.336
Phantom units become missing items (L3).

645
00:44:59.686 --> 00:45:00.603
Watch out:

646
00:45:00.883 --> 00:45:01.630
9.

647
00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:03.446
Ignoring picker feedback.

648
00:45:03.726 --> 00:45:06.542
The same phantom unit is sold again and again.

649
00:45:07.892 --> 00:45:08.809
Watch out:

650
00:45:09.089 --> 00:45:09.793
10.

651
00:45:10.073 --> 00:45:11.481
No admission control.

652
00:45:11.761 --> 00:45:15.153
Retry storms; buyers who never learn they bought (L4).

653
00:45:16.503 --> 00:45:17.421
Watch out:

654
00:45:17.701 --> 00:45:18.533
11.

655
00:45:18.813 --> 00:45:20.797
Non-idempotent checkout retries.

656
00:45:21.077 --> 00:45:23.615
Sold out shown to a customer who already bought.

657
00:45:24.965 --> 00:45:25.883
Watch out:

658
00:45:26.163 --> 00:45:26.952
12.

659
00:45:27.232 --> 00:45:28.320
Unfair access.

660
00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:32.141
Retry luck decides winners (L4), customers notice.

661
00:45:33.491 --> 00:45:34.366
Part 8.

662
00:45:34.646 --> 00:45:35.990
Interview Q and A.

663
00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:38.072
Beginner.

664
00:45:38.352 --> 00:45:39.376
Question 1.

665
00:45:39.656 --> 00:45:41.555
How do you prevent overselling stock?

666
00:45:41.835 --> 00:45:43.200
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:52.077
Make the check and the decrement one atomic operation, UPDATE stock SET qty = qty minus 1 WHERE sku =?

668
00:45:52.357 --> 00:46:00.677
AND qty 0, and treat zero rows updated as not available, with a CHECK (qty = 0) as a safety net.

669
00:46:00.957 --> 00:46:03.816
Record the reservation in the same transaction.

670
00:46:04.096 --> 00:46:06.229
Never read, check in code, then write.

671
00:46:07.579 --> 00:46:08.582
Question 2.

672
00:46:08.862 --> 00:46:11.763
What is a stock reservation and why does it expire?

673
00:46:12.043 --> 00:46:13.409
Thirty-second answer:

674
00:46:13.689 --> 00:46:18.809
A lease: stock set aside for a checkout until payment completes or the lease expires.

675
00:46:19.089 --> 00:46:25.937
It prevents selling the same unit twice while the customer pays, and expiry returns stock from abandoned checkouts.

676
00:46:26.217 --> 00:46:30.590
The TTL is a business choice between stranded stock and slow payers.

677
00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:32.985
Question 3.

678
00:46:33.265 --> 00:46:34.695
What is the checkout flow?

679
00:46:34.975 --> 00:46:36.340
Thirty-second answer:

680
00:46:36.620 --> 00:46:54.092
Validate cart, prices, promos and slot; reserve stock; create payment; on capture confirm reservations and create the order; on failure or expiry release reservations; everything idempotent under a checkout id; implemented as a saga with an outbox.

681
00:46:55.442 --> 00:46:56.509
Question 4.

682
00:46:56.789 --> 00:46:59.882
Why is a flash sale harder than normal peak traffic?

683
00:47:00.162 --> 00:47:01.527
Thirty-second answer:

684
00:47:01.807 --> 00:47:12.965
Normal load is spread across stores and SKUs; a flash sale concentrates demand on one SKU (one hot row) and brings a crowd far above capacity within seconds.

685
00:47:13.245 --> 00:47:20.754
That causes lock contention, pool exhaustion, timeouts and retry storms, and fairness becomes visible to customers.

686
00:47:22.104 --> 00:47:23.107
Intermediate.

687
00:47:23.387 --> 00:47:24.496
Question 5.

688
00:47:24.776 --> 00:47:29.427
How do you handle a single SKU with thousands of reservations per second?

689
00:47:29.707 --> 00:47:31.072
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:47:31.352 --> 00:47:46.499
Split its stock into buckets (N rows) so locks spread; cache a sold-out flag; put a Redis decrement-if-positive gate in front to reject losers without touching the database; or queue reservations per SKU to a single consumer.

691
00:47:46.779 --> 00:47:51.344
Keep the reservation transaction tiny and isolate the SKU's traffic.

692
00:47:52.694 --> 00:47:53.803
Question 6.

693
00:47:54.083 --> 00:47:56.601
SELECT FOR UPDATE or a conditional UPDATE?

694
00:47:56.881 --> 00:47:58.246
Thirty-second answer:

695
00:47:58.526 --> 00:48:07.401
A conditional UPDATE does the check and write in one statement and holds the lock only for that statement's duration inside a short transaction.

696
00:48:07.681 --> 00:48:11.350
SELECT FOR UPDATE holds the lock across at least two round trips.

697
00:48:11.630 --> 00:48:16.302
Use FOR UPDATE only when the decision needs data you can't express in the UPDATE.

698
00:48:17.652 --> 00:48:18.740
Question 7.

699
00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:21.367
How do you release stock from abandoned checkouts?

700
00:48:21.647 --> 00:48:23.012
Thirty-second answer:

701
00:48:23.292 --> 00:48:35.687
Reservations carry expiresat; a sweeper periodically marks expired HELD reservations EXPIRED and returns their quantity in the same transaction; confirmation checks expiry too.

702
00:48:35.967 --> 00:48:39.913
Payment events extend active leases when the customer starts paying.

703
00:48:41.263 --> 00:48:42.287
Question 8.

704
00:48:42.567 --> 00:48:46.066
How do you keep prices and promotions consistent through checkout?

705
00:48:46.346 --> 00:48:47.711
Thirty-second answer:

706
00:48:47.991 --> 00:49:01.879
Recompute prices and promos server-side at checkout, show changes before payment, reserve coupon budgets like stock, and snapshot final line prices, taxes and discounts into the order for invoices and refunds.

707
00:49:03.229 --> 00:49:04.296
Question 9.

708
00:49:04.576 --> 00:49:07.307
What's phantom inventory and how do you deal with it?

709
00:49:07.587 --> 00:49:08.952
Thirty-second answer:

710
00:49:09.232 --> 00:49:14.523
Recorded stock that isn't physically on the shelf (damage, wastage, misplacement, theft).

711
00:49:14.803 --> 00:49:17.085
Checkout sells it; pickers can't find it.

712
00:49:17.365 --> 00:49:26.219
Mitigate with per-SKU buffers learned from pick failures, immediate record correction when a pick fails, cycle counts, and substitution options.

713
00:49:27.569 --> 00:49:28.337
Senior.

714
00:49:28.617 --> 00:49:29.662
Question 10.

715
00:49:29.942 --> 00:49:32.246
Design admission control for a flash sale.

716
00:49:32.526 --> 00:49:33.891
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:49:34.171 --> 00:49:52.262
A waiting room in front of checkout: customers get signed tickets in arrival order (with a randomised pre-queue for those who arrive before start), are admitted at a rate the backend sustains (adjusted from latency), poll with server-suggested intervals, and check out idempotently when admitted.

718
00:49:52.542 --> 00:49:54.995
Add per-customer limits and bot protection.

719
00:49:56.345 --> 00:49:57.476
Question 11.

720
00:49:57.756 --> 00:50:00.849
A payment completes after its reservation expired.

721
00:50:01.129 --> 00:50:02.068
What happens?

722
00:50:02.348 --> 00:50:03.713
Thirty-second answer:

723
00:50:03.993 --> 00:50:14.468
The checkout saga handles it explicitly: try to re-reserve the items; if available, confirm the order; if not, refund automatically and notify the customer.

724
00:50:14.748 --> 00:50:18.055
Never confirm without stock, and never ignore the payment.

725
00:50:19.405 --> 00:50:20.493
Question 12.

726
00:50:20.773 --> 00:50:24.634
How do you keep a hot SKU from slowing down every other checkout?

727
00:50:24.914 --> 00:50:26.279
Thirty-second answer:

728
00:50:26.559 --> 00:50:39.765
Bulkheads: separate connection pools or even a separate service path for flash SKUs, admission control in front of them, timeouts shorter than the pool wait, and a gate that rejects losers without touching the database.

729
00:50:40.045 --> 00:50:42.669
Normal SKUs keep their own capacity.

730
00:50:44.019 --> 00:50:45.299
Question 13.

731
00:50:45.579 --> 00:50:48.053
How would you use Redis in inventory, safely?

732
00:50:48.333 --> 00:50:49.699
Thirty-second answer:

733
00:50:49.979 --> 00:51:07.131
As a front-gate or cache, not the source of truth: seed counts from the database, decrement atomically with Lua, write winners durably to the database, reconcile gate and database periodically (and after crashes), and fall back to database-only if Redis is unavailable.

734
00:51:08.481 --> 00:51:09.675
Question 14.

735
00:51:09.955 --> 00:51:11.918
How do you make checkout retries safe?

736
00:51:12.198 --> 00:51:13.563
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:51:13.843 --> 00:51:30.185
An idempotency key per checkout attempt stored with its outcome: retries return the original result (order created, or failure reason) rather than re-running reservation or payment; downstream calls carry the checkout id; consumers dedupe by it.

738
00:51:31.535 --> 00:51:32.431
Architect.

739
00:51:32.711 --> 00:51:33.905
Question 15.

740
00:51:34.185 --> 00:51:40.671
Design checkout and inventory reservation for a 300-store quick-commerce platform end to end.

741
00:51:40.951 --> 00:51:42.316
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:51:42.596 --> 00:51:56.463
Inventory per dark store with atomic conditional updates; buckets and a Redis gate for hot SKUs; reservation leases with short TTLs extended on payment start; a checkout saga with idempotency,

743
00:51:56.743 --> 00:52:09.009
outbox and explicit late-capture handling; per-SKU buffers and picker feedback; waiting rooms and bulkheads for flash sales; observability on contention, leases, shortfalls and fairness.

744
00:52:10.359 --> 00:52:11.554
Question 16.

745
00:52:11.834 --> 00:52:14.415
How do you estimate and load-test a flash sale?

746
00:52:14.695 --> 00:52:16.061
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:52:16.341 --> 00:52:36.565
Estimate peak arrivals from audience times click-through times time window, per SKU and per store; compute required reservation rate versus per-row capacity; load-test with the real crowd shape (a spike in seconds, retries with timeouts), not a ramp; verify admission, bulkheads and fairness; rehearse the runbook.

748
00:52:37.915 --> 00:52:39.259
Question 17.

749
00:52:39.539 --> 00:52:42.355
Reserve inventory at add-to-cart or at checkout?

750
00:52:42.635 --> 00:52:44.000
Thirty-second answer:

751
00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:50.360
At checkout for most grocery flows: add-to-cart reservations strand stock for browsing customers.

752
00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:55.739
Exceptions: extremely scarce items where a short cart hold improves experience.

753
00:52:56.019 --> 00:53:01.139
In both cases, use leases with short TTLs and show availability honestly.

754
00:53:02.489 --> 00:53:03.662
Question 18.

755
00:53:03.942 --> 00:53:08.038
How do inventory accuracy and the checkout connect organisationally?

756
00:53:08.318 --> 00:53:09.683
Thirty-second answer:

757
00:53:09.963 --> 00:53:18.859
Accuracy is a joint data loop: dark-store ops (counts, wastage recording, picker scans) feed inventory; checkout consumes it with buffers.

758
00:53:19.139 --> 00:53:29.081
Define shared metrics (pick shortfall rate, cycle-count variance), give ops tools to correct stock quickly, and make buffer policy a joint business decision.

759
00:53:30.431 --> 00:53:31.668
Question 19.

760
00:53:31.948 --> 00:53:33.697
How do you make flash sales fair?

761
00:53:33.977 --> 00:53:35.343
Thirty-second answer:

762
00:53:35.623 --> 00:53:49.617
A waiting room with arrival-order admission (randomised among pre-start arrivals), per-customer limits, bot and multi-account detection, signed tickets, and transparent positions; no advantage from faster retries.

763
00:53:49.897 --> 00:53:53.161
Measure fairness (buyers' arrival ranks) and publish rules.

764
00:53:54.511 --> 00:53:55.621
Question 20.

765
00:53:55.901 --> 00:53:57.991
What metrics prove checkout is healthy?

766
00:53:58.271 --> 00:53:59.637
Thirty-second answer:

767
00:53:59.917 --> 00:54:18.989
Checkout success rate and latency; reservation lock waits per SKU; held or expired or confirmed ratios; stranded stock and late captures; missing-item rate at picking; oversell (must be 0); waiting-room time-to-answer and fairness during sales; conversion from cart to order.

768
00:54:20.339 --> 00:54:21.235
Part 9.

769
00:54:21.515 --> 00:54:22.923
Summary and Practice.

770
00:54:24.173 --> 00:54:25.261
Key takeaways.

771
00:54:25.541 --> 00:54:31.706
Atomic conditional updates in tiny transactions; never hold locks across round trips (L1).

772
00:54:31.986 --> 00:54:38.023
Hot SKUs: buckets + sold-out flag + Redis gate; the database stays authoritative (L1).

773
00:54:38.303 --> 00:54:44.917
Reservations are leases: short TTL, extend on intent, explicit late-capture path (L2).

774
00:54:45.197 --> 00:54:49.826
Recorded stock is an estimate: learned buffers and picker feedback (L3).

775
00:54:50.106 --> 00:54:56.165
Flash crowds need a waiting room for survival and fairness; retries must be idempotent (L4).

776
